673:
(a) New structures not completed or otherwise in existence as of June 19, 2018 shall be equipped with a key lockbox at or near the main entrance or other such location required by the fire marshal before a certificate of occupancy or business license shall be issued.
(b) Existing structures that are classified as any of the following shall be equipped with a key lockbox at or near the main entrance or such other location required by the fire marshal on or before January 1, 2019:
(1) Commercial or industrial structures protected by an automatic fire alarm system and/or automatic suppression system that is secured in a manner that restricts access during an emergency;
(2) Multifamily residential structures comprised of three or more units in which access to the building or common areas or mechanical or electrical rooms within the building is denied through locked doors;
(3) Commercial structures comprised of three or more units;
(4) Any building or facility containing any quantity of hazardous materials, which would require compliance with Title III of SARA (Superfund Amendment Reauthorization Act);
(5) Nursing care facilities;
(6) Governmental structures;
(7) Communities in which access to the community is denied through locked gates;
(8) Educational and daycare facilities.
(c) All newly constructed structures, communities, or complexes subject to this section shall have the Knox-Box, a 1.5″ to 2.5″ Locking FDC Plug, and/or means to access community gates, operational prior to the issuance of a certificate of occupancy.
(d) All structures, communities, or complexes in existence shall have the Knox-Box, a 1.5" to 2.5" Locking FDC Plug, and/or means to access community gates, installed and operational within 90 days of being notified of the ordinance from which this section is derived.
(e) The fire marshal shall designate the type of Knox-Box, a 1.5″ to 2.5″ Locking FDC Plug, and/or means to access community gates, to be implemented within the city and shall have the authority to require all structures to use the designated system.
(f) The owner or operator of a structure required to have a KNOX key lockbox shall, at all times, keep keys in the lockbox that will allow for access to the following:
(1) Keys to locked points of egress, whether on the interior or exterior of such buildings;
(2) Keys to locked mechanical equipment rooms;
(3) Keys to locked electrical rooms;
(4) Key to elevator controls;
(5) Keys to the other areas as directed by the City of Centerville Fire Department.
(g) The fire marshal shall be authorized to implement rules and regulations for the use of the Knox-Box, a 1.5″ to 2.5″ Locking FDC Plug, and/or means to access community gates.
(h) Any person who owns or operates a structure, community, or complex subject to this section shall be subject to the penalties set forth in all sections of this Code for any violation of this section; provided that the maximum fine for a conviction for a violation of this section shall be $500.00.
(i) All requests for the designated Knox-Box, a 1.5" to 2.5" Locking FDC Plug, and/or means to access community gates, shall be coordinated through the fire marshal's office and have the location approved on each site.
(j) This article shall take effect immediately upon its passage.
(Ord. No. 2018-3, 6-19-2018)

688:
The police chief shall have the following duties:
(1) To attend upon request meetings of the city council;
(2) To see to the proper service of all summonses, citations, executions, and attachments;
(3) To see that the ordinances, rules, and regulations of the city and all statutes applicable therein are faithfully enforced;
(4) To preserve the public peace, prevent infractions of the law, and arrest violators thereof;
(5) To protect the rights of persons and property;
(6) To hire officers qualified pursuant to the provisions of this chapter, and to discipline police officers, up to and including termination of employment, subject to review by the public safety director and the city administrator;
(7) To oversee the conduct of the officers and men of the police force, and to be held strictly responsible for such conduct and for the general good order of the department;
(8) To keep adequate records of all activities and investigations of the department;
(9) To submit a monthly report to the public safety director outlining the number and type of arrests for state offenses, the number and type of cases involving violations of municipal ordinances, and the number of arrests made and cases reported by each police officer;
(10) To submit monthly to the state department of public safety the uniform crime report prescribed by state law O.C.G.A. § 35-3-36.
(11) To designate, when necessary, certain vehicles as emergency vehicles, provided that such vehicles are registered with the police and in the records of the city; and
(12) To discharge such other duties as may be required of him by the public safety director.

689:
Any person employed by the city as a police officer shall have the following qualifications:
(1) Be at least 18 years of age;
(2) Be a citizen of the United States and a resident of the city,
(3) Have a high school diploma or its recognized equivalent;
(4) Not have been convicted, by any state or by the federal government, of any crime, the punishment for which could have been imprisonment in a federal or state prison or institution; nor shall he have been convicted of sufficient misdemeanors to establish a pattern of disregard for the law;
(5) Be fingerprinted and a search made of local, state, and national fingerprint files to disclose any criminal records;
(6) Possess good moral character as determined by investigation procedure established by the state peace officers standards and training council;
(7) Have an oral interview with the police chief or his designee and the Public Safety Director or a designated interview panel, to determine such things as applicant's appearance, background, and ability to communicate;
(8) Be found, after examination by licensed professional approved by the city to conduct physical and psychological examinations and evaluations, polygraph tests, and voice stress analyses, to be free from any physical, emotional, or mental conditions that might adversely affect his exercising the powers or duties of a police officer;
(9) Complete satisfactorily at least a basic course of 114 hours of instruction within 12 months of the date of his appointment as a police officer, except that an extension may be granted by the state peace officers standards and training council on account of illness, injury, military service, or other reasons deemed sufficient by the council; and
(10) Hold no other job without the written permission of the police chief.

701:
In the enforcement of this article, the public officer and public authority shall be authorized to:
(1) Investigate the dwelling conditions in the city in order to determine which dwellings, buildings, or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use or are vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;
(2) Administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(3) Enter upon a premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in the manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(4) Delegate any of his functions and powers as is permitted by this article or otherwise specifically authorized by the city council.
